WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
When I teach Algebra One, I use what I
call ladder lessons.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.680
I start with a skill my students already
need, and we use it to reach

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.680
the next one. Today, that starts with a
strip of paper.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.600
I put an equation on the board. X plus
three fourths equals one.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.400
Before anybody solves it, I ask, “How sure
are you that you could do

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.760
this on your own?”

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.400
The boy in the yellow sweatshirt is pretty
sure.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.280
He subtracts three fourths from both sides
and writes one half.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.040
The girl beside him is barely sure at all.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.160
She writes one fourth, and she can show me
why.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.000
If you teach Algebra One, you've probably
had a moment like this.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.600
Where would you start with him? He already
knows what to subtract.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.720
Klee-Schramm and colleagues describe this
in their review of algebra

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.040
difficulties. A student can follow a
procedure and still be working out

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.680
what the numbers mean.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.640
So I ask him to put three fourths on a
line from zero to one.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.120
Where would it go? He marks a spot.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.880
I ask him to talk me through it.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.280
Booth and colleagues found that how
accurately Algebra One students

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.160
estimated fraction sizes predicted how
much they learned in an equation

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.080
unit. His mark gives me a way into the
conversation.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.893
I lay the paper on his desk and fold it
into four equal parts.

00:01:13.893 --> 00:01:17.280
When he opens it up, I run my finger along
each space between the

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.520
creases. Payne and colleagues found
explicit teaching and visual models

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.560
were common in interventions for high
school students who struggle with

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.640
math. That's what I'm doing here. I'm
showing him exactly what counts as

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.720
a fourth.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.000
Now he starts at three fourths. I ask,
“How much farther to one?”

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.907
He touches the last space. One fourth.

00:01:36.907 --> 00:01:40.640
We put that beside his equation. He keeps
the subtraction step and

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.080
changes his answer. This time he can tell
me why.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.560
I want to know whether that explanation
will stick.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.320
Prosser and Bismarck studied a hands-on
square-root lesson in an Algebra

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.520
One inclusion class. Students scored
higher on a later check, even

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:56.880
though most felt the model hadn't helped.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.360
So I'll ask him how the strip felt today.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.040
Tomorrow I'll give him a fresh problem and
see what he remembers.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.120
For the next check, I write x plus five
eighths equals one.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.400
Again, both students rate how sure they
are before they write their

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.000
answers. A one means, “I'm really unsure.”

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.120
A four means, “I think I can solve this on
my own.”

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.520
That's the idea behind self-efficacy.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.320
I'm asking about this particular equation.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.760
Watch what happens if I lay a yardstick on
the desk and stretch five

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.760
eighths of a yard of ribbon along it.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.120
The girl can point to the missing length.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.240
Would she feel equally sure looking at the
symbols alone?

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.120
In a study of linear functions, Siefer and
colleagues found that

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.400
students judged their ability differently
when a problem came with a

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.880
real-life situation.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.520
Even the way I ask for an answer matters.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.760
Pajares and Miller found that middle
schoolers reported similar

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.840
confidence when they chose an answer and
when they wrote one.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.120
But with written answers, scores were
lower and confidence was a poorer

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.800
guide to performance. That's why I use
written answers on both of my

00:02:58.800 --> 00:02:59.600
checks.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.560
Now look back at our two students.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.360
With him, I want to see whether he can
find that missing distance on a

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.560
new number line. With her, I want to
return to something she did right.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.600
I tell her, “You found the missing fourth,
and you checked it by adding

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.280
it back. What could you check the same way
with eighths?”

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.960
Zakariya's review describes how successful
practice and specific

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.160
feedback can build confidence. Now she has
a reason to trust her next

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:25.920
step.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.080
She shows her partner how she checked.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.240
He points to the gap he missed earlier.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.360
Granello and colleagues found that working
together could strengthen

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.760
middle schoolers' beliefs about their math
ability, while confidence and

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.200
achievement didn't always change together.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.600
So after they've talked, I give each of
them a new equation to try

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:43.560
alone.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.840
When I look at their work tomorrow, I'll
put each student's prediction

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.800
beside their explanation. Can he show what
the fraction means?

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.800
Can she recognize when her reasoning is
sound?

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.760
Those answers tell me where to start the
next ladder lesson.
